              <p class=3D"corner-intro">A collection of web design and deve=
lopment news and thoughts from CodePen's own Chris Coyier.</p>=0D=0A       =
                   </mj-raw>=0D=0A                          <h3>A11Y</h3>=
=0A<p>I=E2=80=99ve got a couple of accessibility related articles I thought=
 I=E2=80=99d share this week. I=E2=80=99ve specifically picked them out as =
examples of well-done accessibility writing. For each, I=E2=80=99ve plucked=
 out a bit I found particularly interesting. But <em>you</em>, you should r=
ead the whole thing.</p>=0A=0A<ul>=0A<li><a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmai=
l2.com/f/a/sW5fiA3QTRjprlnxz5h2YQ~~/AABEfhA~/FVt37EbrsXlGlzrjTj7wDguikieTvL=
S_rkN__GR9NKP0qmQLzAOP4q8uyMlVdZtLc7Ic3yHeYDa9exx5kzGS0Dxto6Noq0LfzbpBUbb2A=
vyfzpM6ssjDZKrzNHzxYhXUNvpFrUv-TWfQaLMz98y9XCIemlhA4ndppfdAs57KYkg~">Demyst=
ifying WCAG 2.2</a> by Nathan Schmidt. WCAG 2.2 was released late 2023 (and=
 so was this article), but I=E2=80=99m pretty sure 2.2 is the latest stable=
 recommendation, with 3 still being experimental. WCAG is a =E2=80=9Ctechni=
cal standard=E2=80=9D, like other web specs, so it=E2=80=99s not written fo=
r regular website developers like me or you. Nathan=E2=80=99s article, howe=
ver, is and it covers what=E2=80=99s new about 2.2. I learned that those op=
tions on forms like =E2=80=9CShipping address same as billing address=3F th=
at prevent you from having to enter the same information twice are actually=
 <em>required</em>.</li>=0A<li><a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a=
/TUYiwTGH_DP9cjs88iJQ_A~~/AABEfhA~/TBRmpjwDisexmvwB3XnSfJLnAem2MywV4UgJw8G7=
leqLKw_TuL7JpjxkUy0rP-D4pzIrPA4DxaGaF1i8LCl1-2YH0Gc1Ns3iKOPRzZJDCHLf8A5jO9e=
YbSoMXM8liJdzmJgfU-E2C-A8hJXlCwQmNPw1-_OdF1_x5dJQxn9ArLw7rI1RmaJ2_kkd9rzv1R=
btloNT9rhO1uVjK3K0jBYezw~~">Super short note on CSS text</a> by Steve Faulk=
ner. A good reminder that not all screen readers behave the same. Like with=
 <code>&lt;span&gt;=3F&lt;/span&gt;</code> with <code>span::before { conten=
t: &quot;=3F&quot;; }</code>=E2=80=9CJAWS announces =E2=80=9Cquestion mark,=
 question mark=E2=80=9D NVDA does not announce anything.=E2=80=9D</li>=0A<l=
i><a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/XMRCrHN56YlAzsXzDtd8CA~~/AAB=
EfhA~/ENEoMWw9lkwIhxT_HOmCRV0LwVU4QBgLmBEApnyCOfnfUQzHo3s8hkfEbmWbaO9WMAu6p=
3U4Emf33Yw5gx5a7gZXZfuOIwHJ6c75yyD1LOqAw0fBSDRghZsGoAgcte5rCLsa28zHBJlDgqZV=
wAlhtRFOMeoRWNg3_4ToF7cMxxg~">Screenreader only</a> by Donnie D=E2=80=99Ama=
to. Donnie doesn=E2=80=99t love utility classes in CSS. Even setups that do=
n=E2=80=99t use them generally still typically have a <code>.screenreader-o=
nly</code> or <code>.visually-hidden</code>. But what if that was a compone=
nt, what would the semantics of it be=3F</li>=0A<li><a href=3D"https://go.s=
parkpostmail2.com/f/a/jogwB_YAs7xdh-QL4p4g-g~~/AABEfhA~/q3GbHN4qGWX8Bx-mdsp=
05wptShGum3xkvRayNhAHMBqaT_D46UVbDdOJJ_UGJ3UhoJed1CiJNJgnrFR9ixQnQWpRwDu9Uz=
kE_aVDeOwH3RVXujeFehJFxmz7hDlsiIYvjHCRRE0G_V4hWVRFth6gw59yfxe6J7papfDJgkdgF=
pE~">Content hiding techniques and their accessibility implications</a> by =
Sara Soueidan. Speaking of those screenreader-only still utilities, we spec=
ifically use those because other methods of hiding have side effects we don=
=E2=80=99t want. For example, a bit of text for screenreaders-only (and thu=
s visually hidden) can=E2=80=99t use <code>display: none;</code> or it will=
 be hidden from the accessibility tree as well, making it useless. We can=
=E2=80=99t use <code>visibility: hidden;</code> either for the same reason =
<em>and</em> it would still take up space in the flow. There are a lot more=
 hiding techniques as well, and Sara compares, as the title says, their acc=
essibility implications.</li>=0A<li><a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.co=
m/f/a/1RNwZRvJPTDvAK3fPCiOlw~~/AABEfhA~/1pYMjMi6ImiCYB28gHBeXQMEeRn373Q489X=
06zHcw0ezmrmc5ivaJ2uvbBYQ7dK8CTOGkIzvQHjTVH80I4qK_yxKcFJ-rQCYkvT_lbnDhL3XfR=
pfB_uzmhsfUDu-jQDY2bikZmhaAv8AzaW3S9TRYLpVQJUncgCdKB5MaRQxvp0~">Data Table =
Accessibility in 2024</a> by Zell Liew. Zell makes a table out of <code>dis=
play: grid;</code> and subgrid=E2=80=A6 and the results were that =E2=80=9C=
Table semantics was not stripped out by screen readers in most browsers. Th=
ey were able to announce table, rows, and columns accurately.=E2=80=9D (wit=
h the very notable exception of Voiceover + Safari.</li>=0A<li><a href=3D"h=
ttps://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/w0uKWJovyumGl6By3kqf-g~~/AABEfhA~/HvlcKTAB=
JRR397aAiqW2jBgAEXaq5kC40SYuA3QQ8Ux7jm1oAgjki7VXV30G9TME_mLPElzAiqw_Dl80i51=
GsXjH9TM2Mn2Qr_7ijUdWEC0WWE_5ToVUUPiqOaWWfvpapSI7lMVbxNCXu8zyFhMXu-NvmlSzLr=
ni159sW_ALC-sv-lx7p6Xt1poLRCrxrIzwyQee6jqugi5PYnhIOqkCH0afWNBpCTyQkmos6xqLD=
zERkIOV8MI1OTMQTrclU3Ol">contrast-color() is a good thing, but also solving=
 the problem at the wrong layer</a> by Eric Bailey. Eric wrote this in 2024=
 but it=E2=80=99s especially notable now as the CSS function just became wi=
dely available last month. Eric wonders, among other things =E2=80=9CWhy do=
es all this have to be the website or webapp author=E2=80=99s responsibilit=
y=3F=E2=80=9D Would the web be better if browsers enforced this automatical=
ly=3F (Probably.)</li>=0A</ul>=0D=0A                          =0D=0A       =
